rking incredibly hard at  the moment to create that plan uh to  ensure that we can get through that  backlog as quickly as possible and let  me just give you a couple of stats  which is that for example you know  after the january and february peaking  covered this year  we actually hit a very very high peak of  the number of people waiting for more  than a year so we hit 430 000 people but  hey look by  october we've actually cut that peak by  a third because we're going flat out so  what we're seeing is that staff are  going as fast as they possibly can to  ensure we get through elective activity  as quickly as possible but we are  working under some real really important  constraints here i mean for example you  know if you talk to an average district  general hospital chief exec you know  they'll probably have on average 500  beds what they're saying to us at the  moment is 75 of those beds are out of  action because they're effectively uh  we've had to take them out for covered  infection control 50 of them are  currently filled by covert patients and  50 of them they've got medically fit  patients to discharge but we can't  discharge them partly because of the  pressure on social care has taken  another big  leap and it's got significantly greater  over the last few months so you've got  175 of your 500 beds are out of action  at a point 35 percent of your bed base  is out of action  in terms of the non-covered care and we  know that in winter we're using 98 99  100 capacity of those beds so it's not  entirely surprising that at this point  because of covid because of the pressure  on social care because of the workforce  pressures that we actually don't have  the capacity in the nhs that we need and  that is one of the real things that  everybody needs to realize we've gone  through a decade of the longest and  deepest financial squeeze in nhs history  so we've not been able to grow our  capacity to meet that growing demand  we're putting huge pressure on our